85
00:11:43,376 --> 00:11:59,524
The first one is a volume bias, where when you measure at sort of the per change level, a change can go to many different sort of endpoints.

86
00:11:59,790 --> 00:12:00,868
I'm gonna call them that.

87
00:12:01,216 --> 00:12:03,856
Places where software gets deployed.

88
00:12:04,174 --> 00:12:08,976
For instance, you can make a change to a low-level library like the allocator or something.

89
00:12:09,294 --> 00:12:14,532
It goes to basically every single server, but also every client that uses that.

90
00:12:15,616 --> 00:12:19,396
so if you make a bug in that, that's sort of like blocking everything.

91
00:12:20,176 --> 00:12:24,100
but you probably haven't made a bug because that gets extra testing and scrutiny.

92
00:12:25,902 --> 00:12:35,684
another issue is batching, where when you make a change, it has a bug, and it gets reverted.

93
00:12:36,416 --> 00:12:44,576
Um it the change that was rolled back and had the bug actually goes to production.

94
00:12:44,656 --> 00:12:47,556
It just goes to production with the fix.

95
00:12:48,336 --> 00:12:58,276
Because we don't ship like change deltas to production, we ship the summation of applying all of those deltas to a starting point.

96
00:12:58,656 --> 00:12:58,976
Right?

97
00:12:59,056 --> 00:13:04,420
So we're applying a batch of changes at the production point, we're not applying individual changes.

98
00:13:05,006 --> 00:13:07,300
And finally, there's a selection bias.

99
00:13:09,584 --> 00:13:18,180
Where candidates with no test failures are going to be inherently faster to production than candidates with test failures.

100
00:13:18,638 --> 00:13:28,772
So if we compare auto rollback candidates, things where the auto rollback was applied to a specific release to everything, it's not a valid comparison.

101
00:13:29,038 --> 00:13:42,500
Because it's going to tell us that things where the auto rollback feature was applied, or a treatment occurred to you, sort of a medical analogy, those patients were always the sick patients.

102
00:13:43,310 --> 00:13:49,504
It's like looking at mortality between people who got your cancer drug with the entire population.

103
00:13:49,664 --> 00:13:53,444
You have to compare it with the people who had cancer in the first place.

104
00:13:55,342 --> 00:14:06,756
So another gotcha here is project level effects, where each different project that you look at has uh has very different configurations just to start with.

105
00:14:07,054 --> 00:14:22,916
And those configurations end up dominating more than anything we do centrally because people have set things up for different reasons, maybe not good reasons, but they have made choices, or at least copied choices that other people have made.

