00:17:10,208 --> 00:17:14,660
so this creates problems from a statistical perspective.

130
00:17:15,088 --> 00:17:21,808
If you are familiar with your stats 101, it's much better to have sort of normally distributed data.

131
00:17:22,062 --> 00:17:39,364
And even though we have a lot and a lot of data points individually, if we just throw them into a t-test and we like compare one group versus another group, unfortunately what happens is the mathematics of the t-test means that small differences will always show up as significant.

132
00:17:39,488 --> 00:17:43,428
So it's not something that's statistically viable for us to do.

133
00:17:43,888 --> 00:17:52,388
So in order to solve for this, I'm gonna use something that's called a bootstrap confidence interval.

134
00:17:53,008 --> 00:18:10,180
And this is a nice technique for our community in particular, because it allows us to not need to rely on a statistical model in order to compare two groups together.

135
00:18:10,926 --> 00:18:23,844
Instead, what we do is we take our sample of data points and we do many s we do many resamplings of these data points, and we apply whatever our statistical estimator is.

136
00:18:24,206 --> 00:18:41,888
For instance, if you want to compare the the average of two groups, and you have your sample from group one and your sample from group two, you resample, maybe 10,000 times from group one, and you compute the average each time, and you sample 10,000 times from group two.

137
00:18:42,128 --> 00:18:44,128
and you take the average each time.

138
00:18:44,334 --> 00:18:48,068
you can look at the distributions of those averages and compare the two of them.

139
00:18:48,462 --> 00:18:59,204
And this technique is called bootstrap resampling, and it gives you a confidence interval that doesn't rely on any assumptions of how your data may or may not be distributed.

140
00:18:59,694 --> 00:19:06,884
And that's a very powerful technique for us where we have weird data often in the software field.

141
00:19:07,630 --> 00:19:11,620
